Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Drop wrong pvarsym() casts in pexpr.factor (fpc 0.99)
License: expat

compiler/pexpr.pas's factor() declares its local `sym' as `pvarsym'
and then assigns it from symtable searches like:

    sym := pvarsym(srsymtable^.search(pattern));
    sym := pvarsym(search_class_member(pobjectdef(pd), pattern));
    sym := pvarsym(classh^.symtable^.search(pattern));

A symtable search returns an arbitrary psym (procsym, typesym,
varsym, ...).  tvarsym and its siblings are unrelated record layouts,
not a class hierarchy -- `pvarsym(x)' when `x' is a `procsym' just
lies about the type.  Native FPC on x86 got away with it because the
surrounding code only reads psym-level fields (`typ', `name',
`symoptions') after the cast, and those happen to live at the same
offsets across all psym subtypes.  The tp2cc C++ emitter renders the
narrowing as a checked downcast onto a foreign record type; the cast
is rejected at runtime.

Fix:
 * Widen the local `sym' declaration from `pvarsym' to `psym'.
 * Drop every narrowing `pvarsym(...)' at the search call sites
   except the one that actually reads a varsym-specific field
   (`sym^.vartype.def' inside the `recorddef' arm of the field-read
   case -- record fields really are always varsym).
 * Keep `gensubscriptnode(pvarsym(sym), ...)' explicit at that site,
   under the `deftype = recorddef' guard from the surrounding case.

Note: unlike fpc-1.0.6, where the declaration was already `psym' and
only two cast sites remained, 0.99 still has the wider pattern (6
sites across factor), so the diff is larger.

--- a/compiler/pexpr.pas
+++ b/compiler/pexpr.pas
@@ -852,7 +852,17 @@
          possible_error,
          unit_specific,
          again    : boolean;
-         sym      : pvarsym;
+         { Was `pvarsym' upstream, but the sites below assigned
+           `pvarsym(X^.symtable^.search(pattern))' -- a symtable search
+           returns an arbitrary psym (procsym, typesym, varsym, ...), and
+           recasting the result as `pvarsym' is simply wrong whenever the
+           found symbol isn't a varsym: tvarsym and its siblings are
+           unrelated record layouts, not a class hierarchy. Widened to psym
+           so those sites stop lying about the type.  Only one use-site
+           below actually needs the varsym subtype (for `.vartype'), and
+           only under a `deftype = recorddef' guard where the field really
+           is a varsym. }
+         sym      : psym;
          classh   : pobjectdef;
          d      : bestreal;
          static_name : string;
@@ -1050,7 +1060,13 @@
                                                { search also in inherited methods }
